<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # It's the Best! – Lesson # 2</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
    </div>

    <table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tbody>
    <tr>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 2</td>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Reading</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 4th Grade (CEFR: A1.2)</td>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> A Walk in the Neighborhood</td>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> It's the Best!</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Reading: reading1: Outline key information in simple factual texts on familiar subjects. reading2: Comprehend instructions for classroom projects and crafts. phonemicAwareness: Decode English graphemes and phonemes using knowledge of word parts, syllabification, and phonemic awareness.</p>
    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Reading: Can read and outline key information about places in the neighborhood in short texts using superlatives (e.g., 'The library is the best place to read.'). Can comprehend instructions and highlight phrases describing familiar places for a classroom project (e.g., 'The park has swings.'). Can blend spoken onsets and rhymes to form one-syllable and two-syllable words (e.g., /b/ and /ɪg/ in 'big' and /b/ /ɪg/ /g/ /əv/) in bigger.</p>
    </td>
    </tr>
    </tbody>
    </table>

    <h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
    <table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
    <tr>
    <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 33%;">Stage & Description</th>
    <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 33%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
    <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 34%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
    </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
    <tr>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
    <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
    </td>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays flashcards of neighborhood places (park, library, school, street, playground, building) that were introduced in Lesson 1. Students identify each place. The teacher then asks, "What is your favorite place?" and models the answer: "My favorite place is the park." or "I like the park because it is fun." (integrating vocabulary: *favorite, park, fun*, grammar: *What is your favorite?*, *I like this because it is fun*).</p>
    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models the pronunciation of key vocabulary from the flashcards such as /b/ in 'best', 'big', 'building' and /f/ in 'favorite', 'fun', 'flower'. The teacher clearly states: "This park is the best place!" or "The library is the best place to read." The teacher also models blending onset /b/ and rhyme /ɪg/ to form 'big', then 'bigger', 'biggest' using gestures to show size changes, emphasizing the sounds for phonemic awareness.</p>
    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding of 'best' and 'favorite'.</p>
    <ul>
    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is 'best' good or bad?" (Expected answer: Good!)</li>
    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If something is your 'favorite', do you like it a lot or a little?" (Expected answer: A lot!)</li>
    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Can you show me a 'big' building? How about a 'bigger' building? And the 'biggest' building?" (Students use hands to indicate size, reinforcing phonemic awareness through visual connection).</li>
    </ul>
    </td>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
    <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "A Walk in the Neighborhood".</p>
    </td>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A short factual text (e.g., "Our Amazing Neighborhood") with simple sentences describing three different places in a neighborhood: a park, a library, and a street. The text includes superlatives (e.g., "The park is the best place to play."), simple present tense with 'because' (e.g., "I like the library because it is quiet."), and target vocabulary (park, tree, flower, building, street, path, playground, fun, beautiful, quiet, best, big).</p>
    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> The students will read and outline key information in simple factual texts on familiar subjects.</p>
    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
    <ul>
    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist Question:</b> The teacher asks: "What is this text about?" (Expected answer: Our neighborhood places / A walk in the neighborhood).</li>
    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False Activity:</b> Students read sentences about the text and mark them True or False. For example:
    <br>1. The park has tall trees. (True/False)
    <br>2. The library is a busy place. (True/False)
    <br>3. The street has small flowers. (True/False)</li>
    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information Matching:</b> Students match a picture of a neighborhood place (park, library, street) with a short descriptive sentence from the text. For example, match 'Park' to 'It has the biggest playground.'</li>
    </ul>
    </td>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
    <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Reading.</p>
    </td>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Reading and identifying superlatives, simple present with 'because', and specific vocabulary (neighborhood, park, place, tree, flower, building, street, path, playground, fun, exciting, beautiful, quiet, friendly, busy, safe, favorite, best, big, small).</p>
    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
    <ul>
    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice - Gap-fill Reading:</b> Students receive a worksheet with a modified version of the text from Stage 2, containing blanks for key vocabulary words or superlatives. They read the text and fill in the blanks with the correct words from a word bank (e.g., *best, fun, quiet, biggest, flowers, playground*).
    <br>Example: "The park is the ______ place to play. It has the ______ trees and many colorful ______."</li>
    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled Practice - Sentence Completion:</b> Students read incomplete sentences describing neighborhood places and choose the most appropriate adjective or noun from two options to complete them. They then read their completed sentence aloud to a partner, justifying their choice using 'because'.
    <br>Example: "The library is the most (quiet/noisy) place because it is for reading." (Students read the sentence and complete it, then say: "The library is the most quiet place because it is for reading.")</li>
    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Interaction - Read & Compare:</b> Students work in pairs. Each pair receives two short reading cards, each describing a different neighborhood place using superlatives and 'because'. They read their own card, then read their partner's card. After reading both, they discuss which place they think is "the best" and why, pointing to specific phrases in the text. (e.g., "I think the 'Blue Street' is the best because the text says it has the most beautiful flowers.").</li>
    </ul>
    </td>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
    <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "A Walk in the Neighborhood".</p>
    </td>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format: Reading Instructions for a Project & Sharing:</b> Students are given a simple set of instructions to create a "My Favorite Neighborhood Place" poster (a classroom project). The instructions include steps like "1. Read about the park. 2. Draw the biggest tree. 3. Write why it is the best place." Students first read the instructions carefully, then highlight or underline the key action verbs and descriptive phrases (e.g., 'draw the biggest tree', 'write why it is the best place'). After reading and highlighting, students work in small groups. They read aloud one instruction at a time and explain in their own words what they need to do, using phrases like "First, I read about the park because..." and "Then, I draw the biggest tree because...". This activity focuses on comprehending instructions and outlining key information, which are core reading skills, and applying them for a future task, reinforcing the theme "It's the Best!".</p>
    </td>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
    <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
    </td>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment:</b> Teacher Observation Checklist. The teacher observes students during Stage 4, focusing on their ability to comprehend and outline key information from the instructions and short texts. The checklist includes items like: "Identifies main ideas in text," "Highlights key phrases in instructions," "Reads words with /b/ and /f/ sounds correctly," "Recognizes superlative forms," "Can identify what makes a place 'the best' from a text."</p>
    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students receive a new, short descriptive paragraph (4-5 sentences) about another neighborhood place. They read the text and then answer 2-3 multiple-choice comprehension questions or identify all the superlative adjectives present in the text by circling them. They also identify a phrase that describes why the place is liked (e.g., "I like the building because it is amazing").</p>
    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
    <ul>
    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Accuracy in outlining key information from instructions or factual texts.</li>
    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Correct identification of superlative forms (e.g., 'best', 'biggest') within the text.</li>
    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Comprehension of basic factual details from the reading.</li>
    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Ability to decode words containing target phonemes /b/ and /f/ (e.g., 'best', 'favorite', 'big', 'bigger', 'biggest').</li>
    </ul>
    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete two sentences on a small paper slip:</p>
    <ul>
    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Today I read about ___________________ and learned that ___________________ is the best."</li>
    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"One new word I can read now is ___________________."</li>
    </ul>
    </td>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
    <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
    </td>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Reflection:</b></p>
    <ul>
    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was the most interesting part or place you read about today?"</li>
    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Was it easy or difficult to find the important words in the texts? Why?"</li>
    </ul>
    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher Reflection:</b> The reading comprehension activities worked well for most students, especially the gap-fill and matching tasks which provided clear scaffolding. Some students struggled with outlining key information in the project instructions; perhaps a visual guide or more explicit modeling on how to identify action verbs would be beneficial for future reading comprehension tasks involving instructions. The use of 'because' to justify preferences based on text was well-received. Phonemic awareness practice with 'big,' 'bigger,' 'biggest' was effective in linking sound to meaning. I need to ensure that the next reading texts continue to reinforce these concepts and vocabulary.</p>
    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to Next Lesson:</b> In Lesson 3 (Speaking & Interaction), students will use the information and ideas they read about today to describe their favorite neighborhood places to their classmates, using superlatives and 'because' to express opinions and justify their preferences in oral interactions.</p>
    </td>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
    </tr>
    </tbody>
    </table>